Thanks Andy and Daniel,

Good idea on quadratics. I’ve got those running now.

Realism—I think the non-linear results that emerge between covariates and density are unrealistic at times. Here’s an example, it’s not egregious, but works. % canopy cover on the x, grizzly density on y. In the simplest case I’d expect as there are more trees, there is less sun hitting the ground, and eventually less food for bears. As a first guess I’d expect this to be linear. Such that 10% 10% more trees (less food) has a 1:1 effect on bear density. But the result is not linear. Density declines by about the same amount between 0-10% canopy as it does between 10-60% canopy.

Extrapolation issues are their own issue, I agree. When you extrapolate outside your covariate space it is well known to create issues. My point was that these non-linear results can make that issue even worse. If I had data on bear density from 7-60% canopy I’d feel like I had decent coverage of the space. But when I extrapolate even a little below 7% I get huge increases in density due to that non-linear effect.

Best regards,
Clayton

